A||||9||86SE||Ported or Sealed subwoofer box??||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||07-27-2000||03:08 AM||||Hmmm, I can't decide.......sealed (which i currently have) is super loud, but dosent hit very hard.<P>Any experiance with ports with the recommended diameter and legnth?  Will it make the sub hit harder (10" 600watt sub in a .7' cubic foot box).....<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||07-27-2000||03:37 AM||||A ported box requires less power and will sound "boomier". Sealed boxes tend to pound harder and sharper. It also depends on the crossover and amplifier you are using. Some amplifiers are not very good sub amps. What amp are you using? What type of music are you playing?||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||07-27-2000||01:32 PM||||Thats a big box! .7cu.ft. my JL and Solobarics lived in .35 with 1 pound of polyfil.. and the Solo would hit 141.3! thats defening... a ported box needs to be larger and the ports should be "...small enough not to waste precisous space and large enough not to whistle.." (Joe Sessem HI-FI Buys masterinstaller)I had 2 12" in my 94 300zx in an Isobaric configureation pushed buy a TINY Kenwood KAC-606 (50x2 @ 4ohms) at 80hz would hit about 130db but would drop to 11hz with out any resonance. 2cu.ft. w/2 4" ports. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 07-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SE||07-27-2000||03:35 PM||||Its closer to .6 cubic feet.  It recommended for a box .7-1 cubic foot, so i'll put in some pollyfill.<P>The amp is a sub amp, its a AutoTeck xi800 800 watt amp.  Sub hooked up the the right channel at 2 ohms.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000004||b lo 0||07-27-2000||04:14 PM||||You are posing a question that has no definite answer.  I have been building installing and selling car stereos for quite some time now (check the link in my sig), and my experience tells me that there is no perfect enclosure.  Sure, a ported box is going to be more efficient, however, the bass will be "muddy," especially at low frequencies.  In my experience, if you have the space, a large sealed enclosure running a lot of power will sound better, but then there's cost (larger amp) and space constraints.  A ported enclosure is good for a budget system or if you're trying to go real loud with minimal space.  What you need to do if you decide on ported is contact a professional (we're talking Audiopro, not Best Buy) installer and get the correct tube length formulas (the length will vary with desired resonating frequency and box size and shape).  The first step in any good sub installation is to find the resonating frequency (I left my chart at home) which is a factor of the volume and length of the passenger cabin.  Again, contact an installer for formulas.  Also, I recommend you get a test CD (I use Bass Mechanik, from Pandisc-they have a lot of test CDs) to fine tune your crossovers and enclosure.  All this sounds like a lot of work, but it will really pay off in the end.  Also, ONLY construct a box from Medium Density Fiberboard.  Any installer worth his wire strippers will tell you that.  Plywood, and especially particle board are VERY BAD IDEAS.  I reccomend you go to <A HREF="http://www.jlaudio.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jlaudio.com</A>  and check out their Tutorial section-it is VERY informative and their techies will be glad to answer you questions.<P>As for me, I run a JL Audio 12W6 off of a Prestige 400 watt amp (very efficient speaker) in a 1.0 cubic foot sealed enclosure packed with about 4 pounds of polyfill.<P>1FST2M6, your Solobarics are designed to work in EXTREMELY small enclosures.  Kicker made them for pickups and small sports cars.  I'll shut my trap now.  Just a few words of wisdom from me.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">  <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!||||reg||3
Z||000005||86SE||07-28-2000||12:53 AM||||I just put a bunch of polyfill in my sub box.  I dident pack it, just filled it.  I can't really tell a difference, it DOES take SOME of the 'echo' in the box away.  Do I have to pack it to hear a major difference??<P>BTW, why is plywood a bad material for a sub box?  I had a car with a system made from 3/4" plywood, and in the Fiero I have 3/4" MDF board, and I have had 0 problems with any of them......<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||5
Z||000006||b lo 0||07-28-2000||10:04 AM||||Plywood and particle board seem like good, cheap alternatives at first, but they flex a lot more than MDF, and, well, just read this.  A friend of mone had an '85 CRX he put a system in.  He put 3 JL 10W4's in a plywood enclosue (3/4")  He had the system in there maybe 3 days when we were in the school parking lot, showing off to our friends, and the box EXPLODED, yes, exploded in the back of his car.  Not fun.  We were darn lucky no one got hit by shrapnel.  The box was decimated.  You probably won't have that problem with only one 10", but I don't loke to take any chances.  A good thing to think is that when you are building a sub box, you want to have it made so well and braced so much that you could use it to prop your car up if you got a flat tire and had no jack.  There is no such thing as a box that's too strong.  Once again, I will shut my piehole before I get my self in trouble (doesn't everyone seem a little touchier on here lately?)<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">  <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||spike||07-28-2000||01:06 PM||||When I built my last sub box for my previous 87 GT I made it out of 1/2 inch plywood. I had to use such thin wood because of restricted room in the Fiero. My box had 2 10 inch Kenwood subs in it. I had made 2 small ports in the box to help the subs breath better. I found out that even though my box was a little small for these subs, when I blocked the ports it sounded way better. If you are a first time box builder I would go with a sealed box. It is easier to build and even if it is not quite the right size it will sound better then a ported box not built to specs. I have not started my new box for my new Fiero because I am debating on the convertable route. If I choose this route I will have to design a new box. Without having a perminent roof my old box won't work.<P>Mike. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||KevinP||07-28-2000||04:43 PM||||b lo has given a lot of excellent info, but you should also remember a few important things. One, not all subs are designed to work in sealed enclosures.  Many subs need the port in order to pump properly.  Second, you not only need a strong box, you need a HARD box.  I've been building boxes for my friends for about a year and one of my tricks is to plaster the inside with bondo and sand it down to a smooth finish.  This not only adds rigidity to the panels, it creates a much harder surface for the sound waves to reflect off of.  As for the poly-fill, you don't need to really pack it in.  It only slows the sound waves down, and makes the sub think that the box volume is much larger.  I always fill my boxes, but you don't need to over do it.  That's my two cents!<BR>Latta'<P>Kevin<BR>12" Cerwin-Vega pumpin' @ 800 watts||||reg||7
Z||000009||86SE||07-28-2000||06:51 PM||||Thanks for all the info!!<P>Right now the sub is facing the floor which has about 3 layers of sound absorbing material (floormat, carpet, whatever is under the carpet).  I'll put an alluminum plate directly in the fireing range of the sub.  That should increase the noise level!  Hopefully........<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||10
